syslog(3) writes a message that /dev/log does not take where the
system's log takes it (ADR 0506), and prints nothing of its own. When it
could not connect to /dev/log, it printed "mlibc: syslog: connect
returned an error, falling back to infoLogger" in red on standard error,
then every message there after "mlibc: syslog: ", and left out
LOG_PERROR and LOG_CONS. It now tries /dev/log for each message, as musl
does, so that a syslog daemon that starts later takes them; then the
kernel log, /devices/kmsg, as one line with the message's facility and
level in front, <N>, as logger(1) writes; then, for a service that does
not see the kernel log, the same line on standard error, which Spark
keeps in the log at that level; and only when none of them took it, the
console, with LOG_CONS. LOG_PERROR copies each message to standard
error, as glibc does, and then the line does not go there as well. The
kernel and Spark put the program's name in front of a line and mark it
with the process, so the line has the ident only when it is not the
program's name, and no process number. Dukovo only.

